TY  - JOUR
TI  - Digital relapse prevention plan for substance use disorders: study protocol for a multicentre randomised controlled trial.
AU  - Salom, Rafael
AU  - Pico Rada, Alvaro
AU  - Munoz Garcia, Juan Jesus
AU  - Garcia-Mieres, Helena
AU  - Artes-Rodriguez, Antonio
T2  - BMJ health & care informatics
AB  - IntroductionRelapse remains a major challenge in the treatment of substance use disorders (SUDs), particularly during follow-up. Digital tools are emerging as supportive resources, but few deliver real-time interventions. This study will examine the effectiveness of a digital relapse prevention plan (DRPP) integrated into a certified mobile application to detect early risk signs and provide immediate, personalised responses. Methods and analysisA multicentre randomised controlled trial will recruit adults with SUD. Participants will be randomised to standard treatment plus a restricted app version (control) or the same treatment with the full app, including automated alerts and DRPP access (experimental). The plan can be activated manually or automatically through smartphone sensors detecting risk patterns. The primary outcome will be time to first clinical relapse, while secondary outcomes will include patient satisfaction with the DRPP, adherence and perceived emotional self-regulation. Findings are expected to provide robust evidence on the feasibility, acceptability and clinical utility of digital relapse prevention strategies. Ethics and disseminationThis study obtained ethical approval (code 25/327) from Committee of Hospital Universitario 12 de Octubre. Trial registration number: NCT07052175. Copyright © Author(s) (or their employer(s)) 2025. Re-use permitted under CC BY-NC. No commercial re-use. See rights and permissions. Published by BMJ Group.
