TY  - JOUR
TI  - Highly stretchable anti-freeze hydrogel based on aloe polysaccharides with high ionic conductivity for multifunctional wearable sensors.
AU  - Xiao, Suijun
AU  - Lao, Yufei
AU  - Liu, Hongbo
AU  - Li, Dacheng
AU  - Wei, Qiaoyan
AU  - Li, Ziwei
AU  - Lu, Shaorong
T2  - International journal of biological macromolecules
AB  - Conductive hydrogels have limitations such as non-degradability, loss of electrical conductivity at sub-zero temperatures, and single functionality, which limit their applicability as materials for wearable sensors. To overcome these limitations, this study proposes a bio-based hydrogel using aloe polysaccharides as the matrix and degradable polyvinyl alcohol as a reinforcing material. The hydrogel was crosslinked with borax in a glycerol-water binary solvent system, producing good toughness and compressive strength. Furthermore, the hydrogel was developed as a sensor that could detect both small and large deformations with a low detection limit of 1 % and high stretchability of up to 300 %. Moreover, the sensor exhibited excellent frost resistance at temperatures above -50 degreeC, and the gauge factor of the hydrogel was 2.86 at 20 degreeC and 2.12 at -20 degreeC. The Aloe-polysaccharide-based conductive hydrogels also functioned effectively as a wearable sensor; it detected a wide range of humidities (0-98 % relative humidity) and exhibited fast response and recovery times (1.1 and 0.9 s) while detecting normal human breathing. The polysaccharide hydrogel was also temperature sensitive (1.737 % degreeC-1) and allowed for information sensing during handwriting. Copyright © 2023. Published by Elsevier B.V.
